<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE SNAP IS UNANIMOUS OR IT DOES NOT HAPPEN (plan 53 §3.5 rule 1, QBX-171).

  The companion of record_snap_pass_wide, and deliberately the SAME distance:
  the locator is 144 frames from an edge there and 144 frames from an edge
  here. The only difference is that the two armed lanes' edges are 288 frames
  APART, so they do not agree on one frame — and the pass is then left exactly
  where it landed.

  WHY UNANIMITY AND NOT NEAREST. A candidate set that disagrees means the armed
  lanes hold different material at that moment. Snapping to one lane's edge
  would move the recording relative to the others, which is the one thing a
  multi-track pass may never do: the lanes were played together and must stay
  sample-aligned with each other. Better a 144-frame sliver on both than a
  144-frame SKEW between them.

  WHAT IS ASSERTED, and why it is not the landing. The landing is 388944 both
  with this rule and on a build with no snap at all, so the position proves
  nothing by itself — it is asserted here as the consequence, but the gate is
  the ANNOUNCEMENT: "no snap" and "nothing was near" are different answers, and
  only one of them is two lanes disagreeing. A refusal that is merely a
  non-event cannot be told from the feature being absent.

  Env, set on the CTest entry: SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_sawtooth.wav,
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_LATENCY_FRAMES=4800, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1; RUN_SERIAL.
  The transport is STOPPED before record-start for the reason
  record_snap_pass_wide's header gives at length: only then is the landing the
  locator exactly, so 144 and 288 mean anything at all.

  ON `main` THIS FAILS on the two assert-log lines: there is no snap decision
  there to announce, unanimous or otherwise.

  FLIPPED BY MODE A, AND PLAN 53 §9 DID NOT PREDICT IT (QBX-180). The CLAIM is
  unchanged - the pass stayed where it landed on BOTH lanes, so the lanes are
  still sample-aligned - but one of the two consequences is no longer a lane
  child. The landing at 388944 is AFTER lane 0's clip ends (388800), so lane 0
  is R0: untouched, and the pass is still a plain clip at `0,1`. It is INSIDE
  lane 1's clip (which runs to 389088), so lane 1 is R1: under mode A that
  folds into one union column and `1,1` no longer resolves.

  The alignment claim is now made on lane 1 through the COMP MAP, and more
  sharply than before. The column starts at lane 1's clip start, 197088, so
  the pass's take begins at column frame 388944 - 197088 = 191856; the map
  answers the old clip below that frame and the pass from it. Two takeAtPos
  assertions therefore pin the landing to the exact frame, where the old
  `startTime` assertion pinned it to a clip's start. Both lanes still agree on
  388944, which is the whole point.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_snap_disagree">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="1" mode="off"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:qxa-record-input:1"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="1" input="audio:qxa-record-input:2"/>

    <!-- Lane 0's clip ends at 388800; lane 1's at 389088. 288 frames apart,
         and the locator sits halfway between them, 144 from each — both well
         inside N, and no two candidates equal. -->
    <place-clip trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="196800"
                duration="192000"/>
    <place-clip trackPath="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="197088"
                duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="196800" duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" startTime="197088" duration="192000"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="1" armed="1"/>

    <set-locator position="388944"/>
    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="1500"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- THE GATE: the disagreement was seen, named and acted on. -->
    <assert-log contains="[REC] no snap for a: candidates" minCount="1"/>
    <assert-log contains="[REC] snap a" minCount="0" maxCount="0"/>

    <!-- ...and the consequence: the pass stayed where it landed, on BOTH
         lanes, so they are still aligned with each other.

         Lane 0 is R0 - the landing is past its clip's end - so the pass is
         still a plain child there, at the frame it landed on. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="388944"/>

    <!-- Lane 1's landing is INSIDE its clip, so mode A made one column of the
         two and there is no `1,1` to look at. The column starts at the clip's
         own start and the pass's take begins at 388944 - 197088 = 191856,
         which the map says to the frame. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,1" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" startTime="197088"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="1,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        takeAtPos="191855" expectTake="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="1,0" takeAtPos="191856" expectTake="1"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
